        <!-- Section 1 -->
        <section class="space-y-4">
          <p class="text-[13px] font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-[#0066cc] dark:text-[#2997ff] font-bold">Section 1 · Neurobiology</p>
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-zinc-950 dark:text-white border-b border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 pb-3">
            The Neurobiology of Generalized Anxiety Disorder
          </h2>
          <p>
            Generalized Anxiety Disorder (GAD) is not a psychological defect of willpower; it is a complex, biologically grounded neuro-affective condition characterized by dysregulation across core neural circuits governing threat detection, autonomic arousal, and executive emotional regulation.
          </p>
          <p>
            Functional neuroimaging studies (fMRI and PET) consistently reveal altered functional connectivity in the <strong>fronto-amygdala circuitry</strong>. Under normal physiological conditions, the basolateral amygdala acts as an immediate threat sensor, detecting novelty or potential danger. The <strong>ventromedial prefrontal cortex (vmPFC)</strong> and the <strong>anterior cingulate cortex (ACC)</strong> exert top-down inhibitory control over this alarm system, signaling safety when an ambiguous stimulus is determined to be benign. In individuals with Generalized Anxiety Disorder:
          </p>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-4">
            <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 space-y-2">
              <span class="text-[13px] font-mono font-bold text-[#0066cc] dark:text-[#2997ff] uppercase tracking-wider">Top-Down Inhibitory Deficit</span>
              <p class="text-[13px] text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">The vmPFC exhibits hypoactivation and reduced structural/functional connectivity to the amygdala, resulting in impaired extinction of fear conditioning and persistent anticipatory dread.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 space-y-2">
              <span class="text-[13px] font-mono font-bold text-[#0066cc] dark:text-[#2997ff] uppercase tracking-wider">HPA Axis Hyper-Reactivity</span>
              <p class="text-[13px] text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">The hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis maintains elevated baseline corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH) secretion, flattening diurnal cortisol curves and perpetuating systemic hyperarousal.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            At the neurotransmitter level, GAD involves complex imbalances within the gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA), serotonin (5-HT), and norepinephrine (NE) systems. Reduced sensitivity or density of GABA-A receptor complexes reduces the brain’s primary inhibitory braking mechanism, lowering the activation threshold for locus coeruleus norepinephrine firing.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- Section 2 -->
        <section class="space-y-4">
          <p class="text-[13px] font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-[#0066cc] dark:text-[#2997ff] font-bold">Section 2 · Dual-Domain Phenotype</p>
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-zinc-950 dark:text-white border-b border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 pb-3">
            Cognitive Worry vs. Somatic Anxiety Manifestations
          </h2>
          <p>
            Anxiety is a multidimensional phenomenon expressing itself along two distinct but interacting vectors: the <strong>cognitive/affective worry domain</strong> and the <strong>somatic/autonomic arousal domain</strong>. Differentiating these components is critical for tailoring therapeutic interventions:
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-4 my-4">
            <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 space-y-3">
              <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
                <h3 class="font-bold text-sm sm:text-base text-zinc-950 dark:text-white">1. The Cognitive Worry Subscale (GAD-7 Items 1, 2, 3, 7)</h3>
                <span class="text-[13px] font-mono text-[#0066cc] dark:text-[#2997ff]">Intolerance of Uncertainty</span>
              </div>
              <p class="text-[13px] sm:text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
                Cognitive worry in GAD is characterized by verbal-linguistic perseveration (“what-if” thinking) rather than vivid visual imagery. According to Thomas Borkovec’s Avoidance Model of Worry, verbal worry functions as a maladaptive cognitive avoidance strategy: by staying immersed in abstract verbal problem-solving, the individual blunts intense somatic sensations. However, this prevents emotional processing and reinforces the belief that catastrophic outcomes were only averted because of the worry itself.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 space-y-3">
              <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
                <h3 class="font-bold text-sm sm:text-base text-zinc-950 dark:text-white">2. The Somatic Tension Subscale (GAD-7 Items 4, 5, 6)</h3>
                <span class="text-[13px] font-mono text-[#0066cc] dark:text-[#2997ff]">Sympathetic Dominance</span>
              </div>
              <p class="text-[13px] sm:text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
                Somatic anxiety reflects prolonged sympathetic nervous system activation and vagal tone withdrawal (reduced Heart Rate Variability / HRV). Symptoms include chronic trapezius and cervical muscle bracing, tension headaches, shallow diaphragmatic excursion, gastrointestinal hypermotility (IBS symptoms), psychomotor fidgeting, and emotional irritability due to sensory overload.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Section 3 -->
        <section class="space-y-4">
          <p class="text-[13px] font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-[#0066cc] dark:text-[#2997ff] font-bold">Section 3 · Clinical Interventions</p>
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-zinc-950 dark:text-white border-b border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 pb-3">
            Evidence-Based Treatments & Nervous System Downshifting
          </h2>
          <p>
            Generalized Anxiety Disorder is highly responsive to evidence-based psychotherapeutic and medical modalities. First-line treatments supported by clinical consensus include:
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-3 my-4">
            <div class="p-4 rounded-2xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 flex gap-4">
              <span class="text-lg font-black font-mono text-[#0066cc] dark:text-[#2997ff]">CBT</span>
              <div>
                <h3 class="font-bold text-sm text-zinc-950 dark:text-white mb-1">Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (Gold Standard)</h3>
                <p class="text-[13px] text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400">CBT for GAD targets intolerance of uncertainty, decatastrophizes worst-case probability overestimates, and incorporates behavioral worry exposure to break cognitive avoidance loops.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="p-4 rounded-2xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 flex gap-4">
              <span class="text-lg font-black font-mono text-[#0066cc] dark:text-[#2997ff]">ACT</span>
              <div>
                <h3 class="font-bold text-sm text-zinc-950 dark:text-white mb-1">Acceptance and Commitment Therapy</h3>
                <p class="text-[13px] text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Focuses on cognitive defusion (observing thoughts as mental events rather than literal truths), psychological flexibility, and aligning daily actions with core personal values regardless of internal anxiety states.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="p-4 rounded-2xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 flex gap-4">
              <span class="text-lg font-black font-mono text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400">MED</span>
              <div>
                <h3 class="font-bold text-sm text-zinc-950 dark:text-white mb-1">Pharmacotherapy (SSRIs / SNRIs)</h3>
                <p class="text-[13px] text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitors (e.g., escitalopram, sertraline) and Serotonin-Norepinephrine Reuptake Inhibitors (e.g., venlafaxine, duloxetine) are first-line medical therapies that enhance neuroplasticity and fronto-limbic regulatory tone over 4–8 weeks.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="p-4 rounded-2xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 flex gap-4">
              <span class="text-lg font-black font-mono text-teal-600 dark:text-teal-400">SOM</span>
              <div>
                <h3 class="font-bold text-sm text-zinc-950 dark:text-white mb-1">Physiological Sigh & Parasympathetic Activation</h3>
                <p class="text-[13px] text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Two quick nasal inhales followed by a prolonged, unforced oral exhale (the physiological sigh) rapidly reinflates collapsed alveoli and engages the vagus nerve, slowing heart rate within seconds via respiratory sinus arrhythmia.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Section 4 -->
        <section class="space-y-4">
          <p class="text-[13px] font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-[#0066cc] dark:text-[#2997ff] font-bold">Section 4 · Differential Diagnosis</p>
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-zinc-950 dark:text-white border-b border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 pb-3">
            Differential Diagnosis & Clinical Boundaries
          </h2>
          <p>
            Anxiety is a transdiagnostic symptom that overlaps with several other psychological and medical conditions. Clinicians differentiate GAD based on specific diagnostic boundaries:
          </p>

          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-4">
            <div class="p-4 rounded-2xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 space-y-1">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-sm text-zinc-950 dark:text-white">GAD vs. Panic Disorder</h3>
              <p class="text-[13px] text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
                Panic disorder features sudden, discrete surges of intense fear with acute cardiovascular symptoms (palpitations, shortness of breath, terror of dying) peaking within minutes. GAD is characterized by continuous, low-to-moderate generalized tension and diffuse worry.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="p-4 rounded-2xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 space-y-1">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-sm text-zinc-950 dark:text-white">GAD vs. Major Depression (PHQ-9)</h3>
              <p class="text-[13px] text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
                While highly co-morbid (up to 60% overlap), depression is defined by pervasive anhedonia, helplessness, and negative cognitive triads regarding the past/self. GAD is driven by threat sensitivity and anticipatory worry about future uncertainty.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="p-4 rounded-2xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 space-y-1">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-sm text-zinc-950 dark:text-white">GAD vs. Adult ADHD</h3>
              <p class="text-[13px] text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
                Both can feature motor restlessness, fidgeting, and task incompletion. In ADHD, restlessness stems from dopaminergic under-arousal and executive dysfunction; in GAD, restlessness is driven by sympathetic autonomic hyper-arousal and fear of failure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="p-4 rounded-2xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 space-y-1">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-sm text-zinc-950 dark:text-white">Medical & Endocrine Mimics</h3>
              <p class="text-[13px] text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
                Thyroid disorders (hyperthyroidism), pheochromocytoma, cardiac arrhythmias, obstructive sleep apnea, and caffeine intoxication can mimic somatic anxiety. Medical workups are standard before formal psychiatric confirmation.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

